# Build an Accessible Image-Upload Progress Component Without Simulated Percentages

## Introduction

Creating an accessible image-upload component with honest progress events is essential for users who rely on screen readers or other assistive technologies. Simulated percentages can mislead users and create a poor experience. This guide walks you through building a real-time progress component that updates based on actual file upload events.

## Step 1: Set Up the HTML Structure

Start by creating a form with an input for file selection and a progress bar. Use semantic HTML elements to ensure accessibility:

```html
<form id="uploadForm">
  <input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
  <progress id="uploadProgress" value="0" max="100"></progress>
  <p id="status">Upload status</p>
</form>
```

The `<input type="file">` allows users to select an image, and the `<progress>` element visually represents the upload progress. The `<p>` tag provides a textual update for screen readers.

## Step 2: Add JavaScript Event Listeners

Attach event listeners to the file input to trigger the upload process. Use the `FileReader` API to read the file and the `XMLHttpRequest` object to upload it:

```js
const input = document.getElementById('imageInput');
const progress = document.getElementById('uploadProgress');
const status = document.getElementById('status');

input.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
  const file = e.target.files[0];
  if (file) {
    uploadImage(file);
  }
});
```

## Step 3: Implement the Upload Logic

Use `XMLHttpRequest` to upload the image and update the progress bar based on the actual data sent:

```js
function uploadImage(file) {
  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
  xhr.open('POST', '/upload', true);
  xhr.upload.onprogress = function(event) {
    if (event.lengthComputable) {
      const percent = (event.loaded / event.total) * 100;
      progress.value = percent;
      status.textContent = `Uploading... ${Math.round(percent)}%`;
    }
  };

  xhr.onload = function() {
    if (xhr.status === 200) {
      status.textContent = 'Upload complete.';
    } else {
      status.textContent = 'Upload failed.';
    }
  };

  const formData = new FormData();
  formData.append('image', file);
  xhr.send(formData);
}
```

## Step 4: Ensure Accessibility

Use ARIA attributes to make the progress bar accessible to screen readers. Add `aria-live="polite"` to the status message and `aria-valuemin="0"` and `aria-valuemax="100"` to the progress bar:

```html
<p id="status" aria-live="polite">Upload status</p>
<progress id="uploadProgress" value="0" max="10,000" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100"></progress>
```

## Step 5: Test the Component

Test the component in different browsers and screen readers to ensure it works as expected. Verify that the progress bar updates correctly and that the status message is read aloud.

## Conclusion

By using real progress events and semantic HTML, you can create an accessible image-upload component that provides accurate feedback to all users.

## Optional background preparation

If the source image also needs a transparent background, [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/) can be used before the workflow described above. Keep an untouched original, inspect the cutout edges on the intended background and check the exported file before uploading it.
